/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-5","number":5,"title":"Capture","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"5.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-5-1-101","code":"5.1","title":"5.1","blurb":"5.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l5-1-1","title":"5.1.1 — Capture records after you play","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.1 — Capture records after you play","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play first, then keep the take with Capture — Note does not use a conventional record-arm pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI recording in Note happens via <strong>Capture</strong>. You play pads (or a MIDI controller) in Instrument View; Note recognizes the input and lets you store what you just played. The knowledge base line is the whole paradigm: play, then press <strong>Capture</strong> after a melodic or rhythmic idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the opposite of arm-a-track-and-hope. The buffer is listening while you try. You decide afterward whether the phrase mattered. Clips are the containers for those kept loops. Automation and Note Repeat are available in both Capture modes; editing (Operations, Quantization, Velocity) comes after notes exist."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can still play without ever committing. If you do not tap <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Create Clip</strong>, or <strong>Add</strong>, the idea is only in the buffer (and <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> may clear it — next lessons). There is no MIDI file import as a workaround. Capture is the highway."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a new or empty-ish Set. Open a Drum Rack or melodic track from the <strong>Track</strong> header — do not add a clip yet.","Stop transport if it is running.","Play an 4–8 beat idea on the pads. Watch notes appear in the clip area.","Tap <strong>Capture</strong> (wording may be <strong>Create Clip</strong> if transport is already running with other clips).","Confirm a clip now exists and the idea loops.","Say the rule from memory: play first; keep what mattered."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How does Note record MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> Via <strong>Capture</strong>, after you play.","What happens if you play but never tap Capture / Add / Create Clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> The notes stay only in the buffer (and may clear if Timeout is on).","Can you import a MIDI clip instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. MIDI clips cannot be imported or exported."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ (“MIDI recording in Note happens via Capture”)","Capture in Note.md (play, then Capture; clips as building blocks)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1 (play pads; Note recognizes input)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play three phrases. Capture only the one you still remember five seconds later. Delete the other attempts with <strong>X</strong> before they become clips, or <strong>Undo</strong> if you already committed."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-2","title":"5.1.2 — Fixed Length vs Free Length overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.2 — Fixed Length vs Free Length overview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose Fixed Length when you want a looping container first, Free Length when you want to play first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clips are created in two modes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Fixed Length:</strong> tap an empty slot (or the plus in the clip area) and pick a length — the manual lists <strong>1, 2, 4, 8, or 16</strong> bars. Play into that looping container. Notes show in the editor/preview; they become part of the clip when you tap <strong>Add</strong>. The Capture KB and the Learn Note overview list <strong>1, 2, 4, or 8</strong> only. Note <strong>1.0.15</strong> (syllabus often says 1.0.16) raised clip length to 16 bars. Teach both: older pages omit 16; the manual plus-icon list includes it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Free Length:</strong> start playing <strong>without</strong> adding a clip, then capture. Transport <strong>stopped</strong>: play up to <strong>eight bars</strong>, then tap <strong>Capture</strong>. Transport <strong>running</strong> with other clips: tap <strong>Create Clip</strong>; the new clip is at least as long as the shortest existing clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"First capture with transport stopped sets tempo in both modes. Automation and Repeat work in both. Overdub of an existing clip is transport running + <strong>Add</strong> — not a third mode name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On a drum track, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> or the plus and pick <strong>1</strong> bar. Play a kick. Tap <strong>Add</strong>. That is Fixed Length.","On a second track, do not add a clip. Stop transport. Play a bass idea. Tap <strong>Capture</strong>. That is Free Length.","Look at both clips in Session. One had a length before you played; one took its length from the performance (capped at 8 bars when stopped).","Write “container first” next to Fixed and “play first” next to Free.","Recite the two commit buttons: <strong>Add</strong> (Fixed / overdub) vs <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Create Clip</strong> (Free)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When do you pick 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16? <strong>Answer:</strong> Fixed Length — plus icon / add clip before you play.","What is the Free Length cap with transport stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> Up to eight bars, then <strong>Capture</strong>.","Which button commits Fixed Length notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2","Capture in Note.md (Fixed 1/2/4/8; Free play then Capture)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars)","Ableton Note.md §3.1 (outlines until Add; Capture vs Create Clip)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make one 1-bar Fixed drum loop and one Free Length melodic clip in the same Set. Label which is which."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-3","title":"5.1.3 — Capture Timeout preference ON (default): buffer clears after pause","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.3 — Capture Timeout preference ON (default): buffer clears after pause","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Capture Timeout on and feel the buffer forget a take after you stop playing for a short time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Capture Timeout</strong> lives in <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Capture</strong>. It is <strong>enabled by default</strong>. When it is on, notes that have not been captured into a <strong>new</strong> clip are automatically cleared after you stop playing the pads for a short period."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is why a great phrase can vanish if you hesitate. The buffer is not a permanent sketchpad in the default setting. The manual does not give the wait in seconds — {type:'gap'} do not invent a timer. “Short period” is the sourced wording."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.0.12</strong> added the setting. The same release removes Capture buffer timeout when <strong>VoiceOver</strong> is enabled — a separate accessibility behavior. Timeout does <strong>not</strong> affect overdub into existing clips (lesson 5.1.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you like to think, then Capture, turn it <strong>off</strong> in the next lesson. Today, leave it on and lose a take on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Capture</strong>. Confirm <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> is on (default).","Open a track with <strong>no</strong> new clip. Transport stopped.","Play a phrase you can recognize. Stop playing. Do <strong>not</strong> tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","Wait through a short pause — longer than a breath. Play one new note or look for the old notes.","Confirm the earlier phrase is gone from the buffer (you cannot Capture it anymore).","Play again and <strong>Capture</strong> immediately so you still have a clip to keep."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default Capture Timeout state? <strong>Answer:</strong> On — uncaptured notes for a new clip clear after you stop playing for a short period.","Where is the toggle? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Capture</strong>.","Which version added it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>1.0.12</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2 Capture","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (Capture Timeout setting; timeout removed when VoiceOver is enabled)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With Timeout on, play-and-wait twice until the buffer clears, then play-and-Capture once. Feel the difference."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-4","title":"5.1.4 — Capture Timeout OFF: buffer survives long pause until play resumes without capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.4 — Capture Timeout OFF: buffer survives long pause until play resumes without capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn Capture Timeout off so a take survives a long pause, and know what finally clears it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> is switched <strong>off</strong>, played notes can still be captured even if you stop playing for an <strong>extended</strong> time. The buffer is only cleared once you <strong>start playing the pads again</strong> and a clip still has not been captured."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the “think, then keep” setting. Walk away, come back, tap <strong>Capture</strong>, and the old phrase is still there — until you play a new note into an empty buffer, which throws the old one away."},{"type":"prose","html":"Still only for <strong>new</strong> clips. Overdub into an existing clip is not governed by this toggle. VoiceOver still has the 1.0.12 special case (timeout removed when VoiceOver is on). {type:'gap'} No official duration defines “extended.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Choose a default for the rest of the course and leave it. Many sketchers prefer off so hesitation is safe."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Capture</strong>. Turn <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> <strong>off</strong>.","On a track with no new clip, play a clear phrase. Stop. Wait longer than feels comfortable (stand up, then return).","Tap <strong>Capture</strong> without playing again. Confirm the phrase became a clip.","<strong>Undo</strong> or delete that clip if you want a clean slot. Play a new phrase. Wait. This time play one extra note <strong>before</strong> Capture.","Capture and listen: the buffer restarted when play resumed.","Leave the toggle off or on as your working default."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["With Timeout off, when does the buffer clear? <strong>Answer:</strong> When you start playing again and still have not captured a clip.","Can you pause a long time and still Capture the old take? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, if you do not play new notes first.","Does off-Timeout change overdub? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2 (off: extended pause OK; clears when play resumes uncaptured)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Toggle Timeout off, play a bass idea, wait, Capture. Then turn it back on and prove a pause loses the next uncaptured take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-5","title":"5.1.5 — Timeout does not affect overdub into existing clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.5 — Timeout does not affect overdub into existing clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Overdub into a clip that already exists and treat Capture Timeout as irrelevant to that pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual is explicit: <strong>Capture Timeout does not affect capturing new notes into existing clips when overdubbing.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Overdub is: you already have a clip, <strong>transport is running</strong>, you play more pads, you tap <strong>Add</strong>. Those new notes commit into the looping clip. The Timeout buffer rules (clear after a pause, or clear when play resumes) are about notes that have <strong>not</strong> been captured into a <strong>new</strong> clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Timeout on or off — overdub still works the same. If <strong>Add</strong> seems dead, check transport (it must be running) and that you are on an existing clip, not an empty Free Length buffer."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a 1-bar Fixed Length drum clip (plus → <strong>1</strong> → play kick → <strong>Add</strong>).","Set <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> <strong>on</strong>.","Start transport. Play a snare into the same clip. Pause a while. Play a hat. Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Confirm kick, snare, and hat are all in the clip. Timeout did not eat the overdub.","Turn Timeout <strong>off</strong> and overdub one more sound the same way.","Recite: Timeout is for new-clip buffers, not overdub <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does Capture Timeout change overdub? <strong>Answer:</strong> No.","What two conditions overdub needs? <strong>Answer:</strong> An existing clip, and transport running, then <strong>Add</strong>.","What <em>does</em> Timeout govern? <strong>Answer:</strong> Notes not yet captured into a <strong>new</strong> clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2 (Timeout does not affect overdub)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (existing clip + transport running + <strong>Add</strong>)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With Timeout left on, overdub hats onto a kick clip after a deliberate pause. If the hats land, you understood the exception."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-6","title":"5.1.6 — How Capture differs from conventional record-arm","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.6 — How Capture differs from conventional record-arm","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Explain Capture in one sentence that would correct someone who is looking for a record-arm button."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A conventional DAW arms a track, rolls transport, and records whatever happens until you stop. Note’s FAQ instead says: <strong>MIDI recording happens via Capture.</strong> You play; Note keeps a buffer; you tap <strong>Capture</strong>, <strong>Create Clip</strong>, or <strong>Add</strong> to keep what mattered."},{"type":"prose","html":"There is no sourced record-arm control on the Note track. MIDI Input says the selected (open) track is armed automatically for the controller — that is input routing, not a tape-style punch. Transport may be stopped (Free Length, first-take tempo) or running (Create Clip, overdub, automation). You choose after the idea, not before the take."},{"type":"prose","html":"You also cannot import MIDI clips to skip Capture. Audio sampling is a different path (Sampler / Drum Rack record). Automation is captured too, but only while transport runs."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a desktop habit makes you search for a red R, stop. Look for <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Add</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on an empty slot. Look for a record-arm button. There is not a documented one.","Play with transport stopped. Tap <strong>Capture</strong>. That is a take without rolling tape first.","Create a Fixed Length clip, play while it loops, tap <strong>Add</strong>. That is committing into a container, still not arm-and-roll.","Write one sentence: “In Note I play, then I keep.”","Write one sentence about what you will <em>not</em> look for (record-arm, MIDI file import)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How does the FAQ say MIDI is recorded? <strong>Answer:</strong> Via <strong>Capture</strong>.","Is the open track “armed” for a MIDI controller? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, automatically for input — but storing notes is still Capture.","Can you skip Capture by importing a MIDI file? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ (MIDI recording via Capture; no MIDI clip I/O)","Note MIDI Input.md (open track armed for input; same Capture workflow)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1 (play, then capture; Fixed vs Free)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Teach the difference out loud in 20 seconds as if a Live user just opened Note. Then Capture one phrase the Note way."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-7","title":"5.1.7 — Clear notes (X) before recapture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.7 — Clear notes (X) before recapture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the X next to Capture to wipe the buffer and try the phrase again before you commit."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Next to the <strong>Capture</strong> button is an <strong>X</strong> icon. Tap it to clear any notes you have already played if you want to start the performance again. The Learn Note overview says the same for Fixed Length outlines: if you play something you do not like, press <strong>X</strong> to clear the outlined notes <strong>before</strong> <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>X</strong> is not <strong>Undo</strong> of an already committed clip. It is the buffer/outline eraser. After <strong>Add</strong> or <strong>Capture</strong>, the notes are in the clip; then you <strong>Undo</strong> or edit. <strong>Clear Capture</strong> is also a MIDI-mappable action, the hardware twin of this control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>X</strong> the moment the take is wrong. Do not Capture a bad phrase “to have something” if you still have time to retry."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a track with no clip. Play a phrase you intend to throw away.","Tap <strong>X</strong>. Confirm the notes leave the clip area.","Play a better phrase. Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","On a Fixed Length clip, play a bad extra layer so you see outlines, tap <strong>X</strong>, then play a good layer and <strong>Add</strong>.","After a committed take, tap <strong>X</strong> and notice it does not delete the stored clip (use <strong>Undo</strong> or delete the clip for that).","Recite: <strong>X</strong> clears uncaptured notes; it does not replace <strong>Undo</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>X</strong> next to Capture do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Clears notes you have already played (the buffer / outlines) so you can start again.","When do you use <strong>X</strong> versus <strong>Undo</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>X</strong> before commit; <strong>Undo</strong> after a change or a committed clip.","Is Clear Capture mappable? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Manual §2.3.5 lists <strong>Clear Capture</strong> among Map targets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (X icon clears notes already played)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.1 (X clears outlined notes before Add)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5 (Clear Capture mapping)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ruin two takes on purpose. <strong>X</strong> both. Keep the third with <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-1-8","title":"5.1.8 — Capture while browser open","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.8 — Capture while browser open","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.1 — Capture mental model</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Audition presets from the browser’s pad view, and treat “Capture while the browser is open” as an unsourced 1.0.12 claim."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You open the browser by tapping an instrument, sample, or effect name. In the preset list you can tap a name to hear a preview, and you can <strong>play the pads in the Pad View below the list</strong> to test the sound. <strong>Done</strong> loads the selected preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus attributes “Capture while browser open” to Note <strong>1.0.12</strong>. The local <strong>1.0.12</strong> release-note extract lists Favorites, user presets, reverse, <strong>Capture Timeout</strong>, High Contrast, name toggles, and VoiceOver timeout removal. It does <strong>not</strong> list Capture-from-the-browser."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official extracts in this course folder do not state that the <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Add</strong> commit works while the browser is open. Do not teach that as a fact. What is sourced: you can play pads under the list to audition."},{"type":"prose","html":"If your install lets you Capture with the list still open, treat that as an observation to verify against a future official line — not as this lesson’s claim."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View. Tap the Track Preset name to open the browser.","Tap two presets so you hear previews.","Play the pads <strong>below</strong> the list. Confirm you can audition on the grid.","Look for <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Add</strong>. If they are hidden behind the browser, close with <strong>Done</strong> or cancel and Capture on the loaded preset — that path is documented.","Load one preset with <strong>Done</strong>. Play and <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Add</strong> as usual.","Write: “1.0.12 extract does not source Capture-in-browser; pads-under-list are for audition.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What can you do with pads while the preset list is open? <strong>Answer:</strong> Play them to test the sound (Manual §7.1.1).","Does RN 1.0.12 in this folder mention Capture while the browser is open? <strong>Answer:</strong> No.","How do you load a preset and then Capture for sure? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap <strong>Done</strong>, then use the normal Capture / Add path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 and §7.1.1 (open browser; pads under the list; <strong>Done</strong>)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (no “Capture while browser open” line in the extract)","Course syllabus <code>note-l5-1-8</code> (claims RN 1.0.12 — conflict / gap)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Audition three drum presets from the pads under the list. Load one with <strong>Done</strong>. Capture a 1-bar loop on that kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-5-2-102","code":"5.2","title":"5.2","blurb":"5.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l5-2-1","title":"5.2.1 — Empty slot → Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.1 — Empty slot → Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start Fixed Length from an empty clip slot so Instrument View is already waiting for a length and a take."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Fixed Length begins in Session. Tap the <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon in an empty slot on a track. That creates a path into Instrument View, where you play, capture, and shape the preset. The empty squares under the track headers are those slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the same entry as Lesson 4.1.1, now aimed at recording. You can also tap the plus at the bottom of the clip area once you are already in Instrument View. Either way you are choosing a container before you play — the definition of Fixed Length."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not start playing on a header-opened track with no clip if you intend Fixed Length; that path is Free Length. Empty slot → Instrument View is the Fixed Length front door."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Session, find an empty slot on the Drum Rack track.","Tap <strong>Add Clip</strong>. Confirm Instrument View opens.","Look at the clip area for the plus / length control (next lesson).","Return to Session with one back arrow. Confirm you are not in the library.","Repeat on a melodic track’s empty slot.","Name the path from memory: empty slot → <strong>Add Clip</strong> → Instrument View → pick length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does tapping <strong>Add Clip</strong> on an empty slot do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It takes you to Instrument View (and starts the new-clip path).","How is this different from playing without adding a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> No-clip playing is Free Length. This is the Fixed Length entry.","Where are empty slots? <strong>Answer:</strong> The empty squares under the track headers in Session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (Add Clip → Instrument View)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (add a clip from an empty slot, then plus for length)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Fixed Length from empty slots on three tracks. Do not capture yet — just arrive in Instrument View on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-2","title":"5.2.2 — Plus icon: choose 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.2 — Plus icon: choose 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the plus icon to lock a clip length before you play."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After you are in Instrument View on the new clip, tap the <strong>plus</strong> icon at the bottom of the clip area to add a clip of <strong>1, 2, 4, 8, or 16</strong> bars. That list is Manual §8.1.1. The Capture KB and Learn Note overview list <strong>1, 2, 4, or 8</strong> only. FAQ and RN <strong>1.0.15</strong> (syllabus table: 1.0.16) set maximum clip length at <strong>16 bars</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Teach the conflict as dated: older Capture pages stop at 8; the manual plus icon includes 16; hard max is 16. If your plus menu has no 16, you are on a build that matches the KB list — use 1–8."},{"type":"prose","html":"The clip then loops at that length. You have not stored notes yet; you have stored a container. Pick 1 for a drum cell, 4 for a melody, 8 for a progression. 16 is the ceiling, not the default."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From an empty slot, enter Instrument View.","Tap the <strong>plus</strong> in the clip area.","Read every length offered. Note whether <strong>16</strong> appears.","Choose <strong>1</strong>. Confirm a 1-bar container is looping (or ready to loop).","<strong>Undo</strong> or delete if you need a different length, then try <strong>4</strong> on another slot.","Recite the manual list: 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16, max 16."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What lengths does the manual plus icon offer? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars.","Why do some pages omit 16? <strong>Answer:</strong> Capture in Note / Learn Note list 1–8; 16-bar clips arrive with the 1.0.15 notes (syllabus 1.0.16).","Does picking a length capture notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It sets the container. <strong>Add</strong> commits notes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (plus: 1/2/4/8/16)","Capture in Note.md (Fixed Length 1/2/4/8)","Note FAQ (max clip length 16 bars)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create three empty containers: 1, 4, and 8 bars on three slots. Do not play into them yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-3","title":"5.2.3 — Play pads; notes appear in editor while looping","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.3 — Play pads; notes appear in editor while looping","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play into a Fixed Length clip and watch notes show up while the container keeps looping — before you tap Add."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Once the clip is added, you play the pads and notes appear in the MIDI Editor / preview. The clip keeps looping until you tap <strong>Add</strong>. The Learn Note overview is more visual: notes appear as <strong>outlines</strong> in the clip preview. They are <strong>not yet part of the loop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That outline state is your rehearsal. Kick on 1, miss the snare, see the ghost of the miss, <strong>X</strong> it, try again. The loop does not grow; it repeats the length you picked. Repeat and velocity still apply while you play."},{"type":"prose","html":"If nothing appears, you are either not hitting pads or you never created the Fixed Length container (you may be in Free Length instead)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Add a <strong>1</strong>-bar clip on the Drum Rack.","Play a kick. Watch the preview/editor. Confirm a note (or outline) appears.","Let the bar loop at least twice without tapping <strong>Add</strong>. Confirm the clip length does not change.","Play a snare. See the second note appear.","Tap <strong>X</strong> if you want to clear outlines and try once more (do not <strong>Add</strong> yet unless you like it).","Describe what you saw: notes visible, not committed, loop continuing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When do notes appear relative to <strong>Add</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> As you play, in the editor/preview. <strong>Add</strong> comes after.","What keeps happening while you play? <strong>Answer:</strong> The clip loops at the length you chose.","Are outlined notes already in the loop? <strong>Answer:</strong> No (Learn Note). They join when you <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (play; notes added in the MIDI Editor; loops until Add)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.1 (outlines; not yet part of the loop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a full 1-bar kit pattern and watch it draw. Loop it twice as outlines only. Do not tap <strong>Add</strong> until the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-4","title":"5.2.4 — Add button commits notes into clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.4 — Add button commits notes into clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap Add so the notes you just played become part of the looping clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Add</strong>, at the bottom of the Pad View, commits the notes into the clip. That is the Fixed Length keep action. After <strong>Add</strong>, the outlines become stored notes. You can play again and <strong>Add</strong> more layers — that is already overdub, and it wants transport running on an existing clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the take is wrong, <strong>X</strong> before <strong>Add</strong>. After <strong>Add</strong>, use <strong>Undo</strong>. First-ever capture with transport stopped still sets tempo here, even though you chose a length first."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Add</strong> is also the button that captures automation later (dotted line → <strong>Add</strong> → solid line). Same label, different object. This lesson is notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Have a 1-bar container with notes visible from playing (previous lesson).","Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Let the clip loop. Confirm the pattern repeats as part of the clip, not as a one-time outline.","Play one extra hat. Tap <strong>Add</strong> again if transport is running. Confirm the layer joined.","<strong>Undo</strong> once if the extra layer is junk.","Recite: Fixed Length keep = <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Add</strong> do in Fixed Length? <strong>Answer:</strong> It adds the played notes to the clip.","What do you tap if the outlines are wrong? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>X</strong>, not <strong>Add</strong>.","Is <strong>Add</strong> also used later for automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Transport running, dotted line, then <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (<strong>Add</strong> adds the notes to the clip)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.1 (<strong>Add</strong> commits; further <strong>Add</strong> overdubs)","Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (<strong>Add</strong> captures automation)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Commit a 1-bar drum loop with <strong>Add</strong>. Listen three times. If it is not the loop, <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Add</strong> a cleaner take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-5","title":"5.2.5 — First-ever capture with transport stopped sets tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.5 — First-ever capture with transport stopped sets tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Let the first stopped-transport capture write the Set tempo from your playing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If Note’s transport is <strong>stopped</strong>, and you are capturing a clip for the <strong>first</strong> time, the Set’s tempo is automatically set based on your performance. The manual states this under both Fixed Length and Free Length. The FAQ says: with transport stopped, play a phrase and add the clip via Capture; Note extracts the tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"“First time” and “transport stopped” are both required in the sourced wording. Later clips do not keep rewriting tempo every take. If transport is already running, you are in the Create Clip / overdub world and the Set already has a clock."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can still set tempo later with the slider, ±1 arrows, or Tap Tempo (4 taps, then continuous). First Capture is the performance-derived method."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a <strong>+ New</strong> Set so you have a first-capture situation. Do not start <strong>Play</strong>.","Glance at <strong>Set Settings</strong> → Tempo so you know the starting number.","On drums, add a 1-bar Fixed Length clip. Play a groove in time with your body, not the click. Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Open Tempo again. Confirm it moved toward your performance (FAQ/manual: extracted from the take).","If you need a known BPM instead, use the slider or Tap Tempo now.","Recite the gate: first capture + transport stopped → tempo from the take."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does Capture set tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> First capture, transport stopped.","Does this apply to Fixed Length as well as Free? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Manual states it in §8.1.1 and §8.1.2.","How else can you set tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Coarse slider, ±1 arrows, Tap Tempo (4 taps then continuous)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 and §8.1.2","Note FAQ (play a phrase + Capture extracts tempo)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 (three manual tempo methods)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a throwaway new Set, clap-play a slow 1-bar take with transport stopped and <strong>Add</strong>. Read the new BPM. Then set 120 with the slider if you want a known grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-6","title":"5.2.6 — 1-bar drum loop Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.6 — 1-bar drum loop Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a complete 1-bar drum loop with Fixed Length and Add."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A 1-bar container is the smallest useful Fixed Length cell. Beatmaking sources treat kick on the downbeat, snare on the backbeat, and hats as the place you show divisions. Build that cell here: plus → <strong>1</strong> → play → <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"You may layer: kick <strong>Add</strong>, snare <strong>Add</strong>, hats <strong>Add</strong>, with transport running after the first commit. That running + <strong>Add</strong> path is already overdub; Capture Timeout does not apply to it. Repeat <strong>1/16</strong> is legal for hats; switch to <strong>OFF</strong> for kick and snare. <strong>Single</strong> is optional if you want upper/lower velocity on the snare. Do not open the MIDI Editor to rescue a take you could replay in one bar."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the loop if it is memorable after a short delay. <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> later if you want a fill version in the next slot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Empty slot on the Drum Rack → Instrument View → plus → <strong>1</strong> bar.","Play kick on the downbeats. <strong>Add</strong>.","With transport running, play snare on the backbeat. <strong>Add</strong>.","Play hats (taps or Repeat <strong>1/16</strong>). <strong>Add</strong>. <strong>OFF</strong> when you finish.","Listen twice. <strong>Undo</strong> the last layer if it harms the groove.","Launch the clip from Session to confirm it is a 1-bar loop."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which length did you lock before playing? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1 bar.","Which button stored each layer? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Add</strong>.","Must hats be drawn in the editor? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Play or Repeat them, then <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (Fixed Length workflow)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (kick, snare, hats)","Capture in Note.md (Repeat available in Fixed Length)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make a second 1-bar clip (Duplicate or new slot) that changes only the hats. Keep both."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-7","title":"5.2.7 — 4-bar melodic Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.7 — 4-bar melodic Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a 4-bar melody or bass figure into a Fixed Length clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Four bars is long enough for a phrase that is not only a 1-bar cell. Plus → <strong>4</strong> → play on a melodic track → <strong>Add</strong>. Enable <strong>In Key</strong> if you want the 5×5 or Keys rectangles to stay in the Set’s scale. Use <strong>Octave</strong> so the figure sits in a bass or lead register."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play the four bars as one idea. Notes show as you play (outlines in the preview until they are committed). If you stumble, <strong>X</strong> and restart; the container still loops those four bars. First capture on a brand-new Set still sets tempo if transport is stopped — on this lab, start <strong>Play</strong> or accept the extracted tempo, but do not fight the click if you already set BPM in Level 3."},{"type":"prose","html":"A 4-bar clip is still under the 16-bar max. You will do 8 bars next. Leave <strong>Note Quantize</strong> alone until 5.4 so you can hear the take you actually played."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track. Empty slot → plus → <strong>4</strong>.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on if you want scale safety. Choose <strong>Pads</strong> or <strong>Keys</strong>.","Play a 4-bar melody or bass line. Watch notes appear.","If it is wrong, <strong>X</strong> and replay. If it is right, <strong>Add</strong>.","Listen to one full 4-bar cycle without touching pads.","Repeat the figure from memory over the looping clip (overdub only if you mean to)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What length did the plus icon lock? <strong>Answer:</strong> 4 bars.","What commits the melody? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Add</strong>.","What happens if you play without <strong>Add</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Notes remain outlines / uncommitted while the 4-bar container loops."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 / §8.1.3 (In Key; Pads/Keys)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (melody / bass as sketch parts)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one 4-bar bass or lead. Do not quantize yet. Leave the human timing for 5.4."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-2-8","title":"5.2.8 — 8-bar progression Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.8 — 8-bar progression Fixed Length","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.2 — Fixed Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture an 8-bar harmonic or bass progression as one Fixed Length clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking advice for chords: find three or four chords that work, loop them until they bore you, then change. An <strong>8</strong>-bar Fixed Length clip is a container for that kind of progression (or an 8-bar bass walk). Plus → <strong>8</strong> → play → <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Eight bars is also the Free Length cap when transport is stopped — here you chose 8 on purpose as a container, so you can rest and retry inside the loop instead of racing a one-shot buffer. 16 remains available on the plus icon if your version lists it; this lab stays at 8."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play in <strong>In Key</strong> if the progression should not leave the scale. You may hold pads with Repeat for a pulsed figure, or play block chords on Keys rectangles. Do not export yet. Structure across scenes is later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Melodic track → empty slot → plus → <strong>8</strong>.","Decide three or four chords (or a bass line that implies them). <strong>In Key</strong> on unless you need chromatic notes.","Play the 8-bar idea. <strong>X</strong> and restart if bar 5 falls apart.","<strong>Add</strong>. Listen to a full 8-bar cycle.","Optional: transport running, <strong>Add</strong> a second layer (higher voicing or a simple lead).","Launch the clip from Session and count eight bars aloud."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why pick 8 instead of 1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Room for a progression, not only a cell.","How does this 8 differ from Free Length’s 8-bar cap? <strong>Answer:</strong> You chose the container first; you are not racing a stopped-transport buffer.","What is the clip-length ceiling if you ever need more? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16 bars."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (plus includes 8)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (3–4 chords; loop then change)","Note FAQ (max 16 bars)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the 8-bar clip. Write the chord or bass movement in four words. That clip is part of the Gate E portfolio."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-5-3-103","code":"5.3","title":"5.3","blurb":"5.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l5-3-1","title":"5.3.1 — Play without pre-adding clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.1 — Play without pre-adding clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start a take by playing pads on a track that has no clip yet."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Free Length means you <strong>start playing pads without adding a clip</strong>, then capture the performance. Open the track from the <strong>Track</strong> header (or sit in Instrument View on an empty clip area) and play. Notes appear in the clip area as you go."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are not picking 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 first. The take’s length comes from how long you play and from transport state (next lessons). This is the improvisation door: the idea happens, then the container."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you accidentally tapped <strong>Add Clip</strong> or plus already, you are in Fixed Length. Back out and use a slot with no container, or <strong>X</strong> and switch mental modes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic or bass track from the <strong>Track</strong> header. Do not tap plus. Do not <strong>Add Clip</strong>.","Stop transport.","Play a phrase. Confirm notes appear without a pre-chosen bar count.","Do <strong>not</strong> tap <strong>Capture</strong> yet if you only want to feel the mode — or <strong>X</strong> if you need a clean buffer.","Compare mentally with last module: no plus, no length menu.","Recite: Free Length = play first, clip second."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you skip in Free Length? <strong>Answer:</strong> Adding a clip / choosing 1–16 bars before you play.","How do you get to the pads without creating a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the <strong>Track</strong> header (or otherwise open Instrument View on a track with no clip).","Where do the notes show while you play? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the clip area / preview."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (play without adding a clip)","Capture in Note.md (start playing pads without adding a clip)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.2 (notes appear; up to eight bars)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play two throwaway phrases with no clip. <strong>X</strong> both. Do not Capture until the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-2","title":"5.3.2 — Transport stopped: up to 8 bars then Capture button","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.2 — Transport stopped: up to 8 bars then Capture button","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With transport stopped, play as long as eight bars and tap Capture to make the clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If Note’s transport is <strong>stopped</strong>, you can play for up to <strong>eight bars</strong>, then tap the <strong>Capture</strong> button to create a clip of that performance. Eight bars is the documented Free Length ceiling in this state — not 16. Sixteen is the clip-length max for clips in general, and a Fixed Length plus option; it is not the stopped-transport Free Length buffer."},{"type":"prose","html":"The button label here is <strong>Capture</strong>, not <strong>Add</strong> and not <strong>Create Clip</strong>. Create Clip is the running-transport wording (next lesson). First capture in this state also sets tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you play longer than eight bars, {type:'gap'} the extracts do not describe wrap, truncate, or reject behavior past the cap. Stay inside eight. <strong>X</strong> if you overshoot and want a clean eight."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Stop transport. Open a track with no clip.","Play a phrase you can count — aim for 2–4 bars if eight feels long.","Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","Return to Session. Confirm a new clip exists and loops.","Open the clip and glance at its length; it should reflect the take, at most 8 bars.","Recite: stopped + Free Length + max 8 + <strong>Capture</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the stopped-transport Free Length limit? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight bars, then <strong>Capture</strong>.","Which button do you tap? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong>.","Is that the same as the 16-bar clip max? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. 16 is the general clip ceiling / Fixed plus option."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2","Capture in Note.md (up to eight bars, then Capture)","Note FAQ (clips max 16 bars — different limit)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a stopped-transport idea between 1 and 8 bars. Count the loop. If it feels like 1 bar of content in a longer clip, that is still a legal Free Length take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-3","title":"5.3.3 — Capture button vs Create Clip wording when transport running","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.3 — Capture button vs Create Clip wording when transport running","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expect the keep button to say Capture when transport is stopped, and Create Clip when transport is running with other clips."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual uses two labels for Free Length commit:"},{"type":"list","items":["Transport <strong>stopped</strong>: <strong>Capture</strong>","Transport <strong>running</strong> and other clips exist: <strong>Create Clip</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Learn Note writes this as “the <strong>Capture</strong> (or <strong>Create Clip</strong>) button.” Same job — turn the freeform buffer into a looping clip — different wording for the clock state."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Add</strong> is not this button. <strong>Add</strong> commits into an existing Fixed Length clip or overdubs an existing clip while transport runs. If you see <strong>Add</strong>, you are not in empty-buffer Free Length."},{"type":"prose","html":"Read the label before you tap. If you wanted a stopped first-take tempo extract, do not hit Play first or you will get <strong>Create Clip</strong> instead."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Stop transport. Play on an empty track. Read the button: <strong>Capture</strong>.","<strong>X</strong> to clear (or Capture and delete) so the slot is free again.","Launch the drum clip you already have so transport runs.","Open a different empty track. Play. Read the button: <strong>Create Clip</strong>.","Tap it. Confirm a new clip appears while the drums continue.","Recite both labels and when each appears."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does the button say <strong>Capture</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Free Length, transport stopped.","When does it say <strong>Create Clip</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Free Length, transport running, other clips in the Set.","When do you see <strong>Add</strong> instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> Committing notes into a clip that already exists (Fixed Length / overdub)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (Capture vs Create Clip)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.2 (“Capture (or Create Clip)”)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do one stopped <strong>Capture</strong> and one running <strong>Create Clip</strong> in the same Set. Write the label you saw each time."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-4","title":"5.3.4 — Transport running + other clips: Create Clip length ≥ shortest existing clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.4 — Transport running + other clips: Create Clip length ≥ shortest existing clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create a clip while other clips play and expect its length to be at least the shortest clip already in the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If transport is running and there are other clips in the Set, you play and tap <strong>Create Clip</strong>. <strong>Any newly captured clips will have at least as many bars as the shortest clip already in the Set.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the manual’s rule. Learn Note says the new clip’s length is <strong>relative to the shortest clip already present</strong>. Teach the stricter manual wording: <strong>at least as many bars as</strong> the shortest existing clip. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not give the formula when your playing is longer than that minimum (whether it rounds to 2, 4, 8, etc.)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Practical effect: a 1-bar drum cell in the Set can allow a 1-bar Create Clip. If the shortest clip is 4 bars, do not expect a 1-bar result from Create Clip. Fixed Length plus is how you <em>choose</em> 1 when you need a cell."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Make sure the Set has a <strong>1</strong>-bar drum clip. Launch it so transport runs.","On a bass track with no clip, play a short riff. Tap <strong>Create Clip</strong>.","Check the new clip’s length. It should be ≥ 1 bar (the shortest existing).","Duplicate or capture an 8-bar clip so the shortest remaining <em>other</em> experiment is longer — or compare against a Set whose shortest clip is 4. {type:'gap'} If you cannot isolate “shortest,” write the lengths you see.","Recite the rule: running + others → <strong>Create Clip</strong> → length ≥ shortest existing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the length rule for Create Clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> At least as many bars as the shortest clip already in the Set.","Which button is this? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Create Clip</strong> (transport running, other clips present).","How do you force a 1-bar container regardless? <strong>Answer:</strong> Fixed Length plus → <strong>1</strong>, not this path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (at least as many bars as the shortest existing clip)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.2 (length relative to the shortest clip)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With a 1-bar drum loop playing, Create Clip a bass idea. Write both lengths. If bass came out longer than one bar, that still satisfies “at least.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-5","title":"5.3.5 — Clear (X) to restart buffer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.5 — Clear (X) to restart buffer","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clear a Free Length buffer with X so the next notes are a new take, not a pile-on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>X</strong> next to <strong>Capture</strong> clears notes you have already played so you can start the performance again. In Free Length that is the buffer reset. The same <strong>X</strong> clears Fixed Length outlines before <strong>Add</strong>. Timeout may also clear (if on) after a pause, or when you play again (if off) — those are automatic. <strong>X</strong> is the deliberate reset, and it works whether the keep button currently says <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Create Clip</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it when bar 2 ruined a good bar 1, or when <strong>Create Clip</strong> would bake a sketch you already rejected. After you <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Create Clip</strong>, <strong>X</strong> is no longer the tool to delete the clip; <strong>Undo</strong> or Session long-press <strong>Delete</strong> the clip instead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Clear Capture</strong> on a MIDI controller maps this action if you use hardware later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Stop transport. Play a Free Length phrase you dislike.","Tap <strong>X</strong>. Confirm the clip area is empty.","Play a phrase you like. Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","With drums looping, play a bad bass buffer. <strong>X</strong>. Play a good one. <strong>Create Clip</strong>.","After the clip exists, tap <strong>X</strong> and confirm the stored clip remains.","Recite: <strong>X</strong> restarts the buffer; it does not delete a committed clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>X</strong> clear? <strong>Answer:</strong> Notes already played that are not yet a clip (the buffer).","Name an automatic cousin of <strong>X</strong>. <strong>Answer:</strong> Capture Timeout (on: clears after a short pause).","What deletes a clip you already made? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Undo</strong>, or Session long-press <strong>Delete</strong> — not <strong>X</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (X icon)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.2 (X before creating the clip)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5 (Clear Capture mapping)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Fill and clear the buffer three times. Keep only the fourth phrase."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-6","title":"5.3.6 — First capture tempo extraction again","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.6 — First capture tempo extraction again","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Extract tempo from a Free Length first take with transport stopped, then compare it to setting tempo yourself."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Free Length repeats the same tempo rule as Fixed Length: if transport is <strong>stopped</strong> and this is the <strong>first</strong> clip capture, Note sets the Set tempo from the performance. FAQ wording: play a phrase, add the clip via Capture, Note extracts the tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do this on a <strong>+ New</strong> Set (or a Set with no prior captured clip) so “first” is true. Play a pulse you can feel — even two bars of kick-like notes on a drum track — then <strong>Capture</strong>. Read Tempo in Set Settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the extract is wild, you still have the slider, ±1 arrows, and Tap Tempo (four taps, then continuous). Remembered last tempo on new Sets is a <strong>2.0.1</strong> behavior; it does not cancel first-capture extract when you actually Capture with transport stopped."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>+ New</strong> Set. Do not press <strong>Play</strong>. Note the starting tempo.","Open drums. No plus. Play a steady pulse for a few bars.","Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> → Tempo. Write the new number.","If you want 90, 120, or 140 instead, set it with the slider or Tap Tempo.","Recite: first + stopped + Capture → extracted tempo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does Free Length first capture set tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, when transport is stopped.","What if transport was already running? <strong>Answer:</strong> You get <strong>Create Clip</strong> / a running clock; this extract rule does not apply.","Name one manual tempo method if extract misses. <strong>Answer:</strong> Slider, ±1, or Tap Tempo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2","Note FAQ (phrase + Capture extracts tempo)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (last tempo remembered on new Sets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Extract once, then Tap Tempo a song you know and compare the two BPMs. Keep the one you will write to."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-7","title":"5.3.7 — Free Length bass idea","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.7 — Free Length bass idea","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a bass idea with Free Length so the phrase can exist before you pick a loop size."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Bass is a good Free Length subject: you find a riff, then keep it. Open the bass track from the header, transport stopped (or running if drums already loop — that is the next lab). Play without plus. <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Create Clip</strong>. <strong>X</strong> until the riff is the one that sticks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>In Key</strong> plus a low <strong>Octave</strong> keeps the riff in the Set’s scale and register. Repeat can pulse a held root; use it only if you want that machine-gun bass. Beatmaking notes treat 808/bass as the tuning reference for the song’s root — land on the root you chose in Key &amp; Scale."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not import MIDI. Do not arm a hidden record button. Play, then keep."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the bass / low melodic track. No clip. Set <strong>Octave</strong> down if needed. <strong>In Key</strong> on.","Stop transport unless you already want drums underneath (if you do, you are early for 5.3.8).","Play a bass idea. <strong>X</strong> if it is not memorable.","Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","Listen. If the length feels odd, you can recapture or later crop in the editor (Level 6).","Play the riff once from memory on the pads without looking at the clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why use Free Length for bass? <strong>Answer:</strong> The riff can appear before you commit to 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16.","Which button keeps a stopped take? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Capture</strong>.","How do you retry? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>X</strong>, then play again."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2","Capture in Note.md","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (808/bass as tuning reference)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Octave; In Key)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep one Free Length bass clip. Write its root note. That clip belongs in the Gate E Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-3-8","title":"5.3.8 — Free Length while drums loop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.3.8 — Free Length while drums loop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.3 — Free Length Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create a Free Length part on a second track while a drum clip is already playing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Launch the drum clip so global transport runs. Open the bass or melody track from the <strong>Track</strong> header. Play without adding a clip. The keep button is <strong>Create Clip</strong>. The new clip’s length is at least the shortest existing clip (often your 1-bar drum loop)."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is how a sketch grows in Session: one looping cell, then a captured idea on the next track, both in time. Metronome is optional; the drums are the click. <strong>X</strong> if the bass fights the kick. After <strong>Create Clip</strong>, you can overdub with transport still running and <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not stop transport if you want this wording and length rule. Stopping puts you back on <strong>Capture</strong> and the 8-bar buffer."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch your 1-bar (or other) drum clip.","Open the bass or lead track. No plus.","Play in time with the drums. <strong>X</strong> as needed.","Tap <strong>Create Clip</strong>. Confirm the new clip plays with the drums.","Check lengths: new clip ≥ shortest existing.","Stop, then launch both (or the scene) and listen once without playing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What label should the keep button show? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Create Clip</strong>.","Why are the drums running? <strong>Answer:</strong> Transport running + other clips is this Free Length path.","What length should you expect at minimum? <strong>Answer:</strong> At least as many bars as the shortest clip already in the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (Create Clip; length ≥ shortest)","Ableton Note Manual §6.1 / §4.2 (global transport; launch clip)","Course syllabus Module 5.3 practice (Free Length over a running drum clip)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create Clip a bass or lead over the drum loop. That pair is the core of Gate E. Do not overdub until 5.4 unless you already understand <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-5-4-104","code":"5.4","title":"5.4","blurb":"5.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l5-4-1","title":"5.4.1 — Existing clip + transport running + Add more notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.1 — Existing clip + transport running + Add more notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Overdub extra notes into a clip that already exists by running transport and tapping Add."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have a captured clip and Note’s transport is <strong>running</strong>, you add more notes by playing pads and tapping <strong>Add</strong>. That is the entire overdub recipe. Capture Timeout does not apply."},{"type":"prose","html":"The clip keeps its length. You are not Create-Clip-ing a new container. Outlines (or preview notes) of the new layer wait for <strong>Add</strong>; <strong>X</strong> throws that layer away. Repeat works. After <strong>Add</strong>, the new hits loop with the old ones."},{"type":"prose","html":"If <strong>Add</strong> does nothing, transport is probably stopped — and stopped + existing clip is the <em>other</em> branch (next lesson): playing can start <strong>new</strong> captures instead of overdubbing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open an existing 1-bar drum clip. Tap <strong>Play/Stop</strong> so transport runs.","Play a new layer (hats or a percussion pad). See the new notes.","Tap <strong>Add</strong>. Listen for both layers.","Play a bad layer. <strong>X</strong>. Confirm the committed clip did not take it.","Stop transport. Notice you are no longer in the overdub recipe.","Recite: existing clip + running + <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What three conditions define overdub? <strong>Answer:</strong> A clip already exists, transport is running, you tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Does Timeout clear an overdub layer you have not added yet the same way as a new-clip buffer? <strong>Answer:</strong> Timeout does not affect capturing new notes into existing clips.","What button is <em>not</em> the overdub commit? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Create Clip</strong> / <strong>Capture</strong> — those make a new clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (existing clip + running + Add)","Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2 (Timeout does not affect overdub)","Ableton Note.md §3.1.1 (continue to Add layers)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Overdub one musical layer onto drums and one onto bass or melody. Gate E requires overdub at least once — this can be that pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-2","title":"5.4.2 — Transport stopped: playing pads can start new captures","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.2 — Transport stopped: playing pads can start new captures","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Stop transport on a track that already has a clip and understand that playing now starts new captures, not an overdub."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have a captured clip and transport is <strong>stopped</strong>, you can start playing the pads again to <strong>capture new clips</strong>. That is the manual’s second branch, right after the overdub paragraph."},{"type":"prose","html":"Stopped means: do not <strong>Add</strong> onto the old loop. You are back in Free Length thinking — buffer, <strong>Capture</strong>, new clip in the next slot path. The old clip stays where it is. This is how you grab a variation without Duplicate Clip, or a different take on the same track."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you meant to overdub, press <strong>Play/Stop</strong> first, then play, then <strong>Add</strong>. Mixing the two branches is the usual “why didn’t my hats go into the loop?” failure."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a track that already has a clip. <strong>Stop</strong> transport.","Play a new phrase. Watch for a buffer / <strong>Capture</strong> (not <strong>Add</strong> into the old loop).","Tap <strong>Capture</strong> if you want a new clip. Confirm the original clip is still in Session.","<strong>Undo</strong> or delete the new clip if it was only a test.","Start transport, play, <strong>Add</strong>, and confirm <em>that</em> path layers the existing clip.","Recite both branches: running + <strong>Add</strong> = overdub; stopped + play = new capture."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does playing into an existing-clip track do when transport is stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> It can start new captures (new clips), not overdub.","How do you layer the clip you already have? <strong>Answer:</strong> Start transport, play, <strong>Add</strong>.","Does the original clip disappear when you capture a new one this way? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not in the sources. You are capturing new clips."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (stopped + existing clip → play to capture new clips)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (running + existing clip → Add)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From one drum track, make a second clip via the stopped-transport path. Leave both clips in adjacent slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-3","title":"5.4.3 — Track-hopping while transport runs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.3 — Track-hopping while transport runs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave one track for another while the Set keeps playing, and capture or overdub on the track you actually opened."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Play/Stop</strong> is global. Launching a clip starts playback on the nearest downbeat. You return to Session with one back arrow, tap another <strong>Track</strong> header (or double-tap a clip), and you are on a new instrument while the clock continues."},{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI Input states the selected (open) track is armed automatically; to input on a different track you return to Session and open that track. Recording still follows Capture: empty track → <strong>Create Clip</strong> if others are playing; existing clip → <strong>Add</strong> to overdub."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not describe a swipe-between-tracks control inside Instrument View, and they do not say Capture follows you without opening the destination track. Hop by going Session → open the next track."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not expect Live-style record-arm on several tracks at once. Note’s documented input arm is the open track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch the drum clip so transport runs.","Back arrow to Session. Open the bass track. Play. <strong>Create Clip</strong> or <strong>Add</strong> as appropriate.","Back arrow. Open the lead track. Play and keep a take the same way.","Confirm drums never required a second Play tap; the transport was already running.","Open drums again and overdub a hat with <strong>Add</strong> to prove you can hop back.","Recite: global clock; open the track you want; Capture/Add there."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does stopping one clip require stopping the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <strong>Stop Clip</strong> is per playing clip; <strong>Play/Stop</strong> is global. Hopping uses the global clock.","How do you arm another track for playing/Capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> Return to Session and open that track.","Is multi-track record-arm documented? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The open track is armed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (global Play/Stop)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (back to Session; launch clip; Stop Clip)","Note MIDI Input.md (open track armed; change track from Session)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 (Create Clip / Add rules)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While drums loop, Capture or overdub bass, then melody, hopping through Session each time. Do not stop transport until both extra parts exist."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-4","title":"5.4.4 — Capture automation of knobs (link L8)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.4 — Capture automation of knobs (link L8)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one knob move into a playing clip so you have seen automation Capture before Level 8."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Automation is recorded via Capture. Transport <strong>must</strong> be running. Move a device or audio-effect knob; a <strong>dotted</strong> line appears in Clip Preview. Tap <strong>Add</strong> to store it. The line becomes <strong>solid</strong>. A <strong>dot</strong> appears by the automated parameter. While transport runs, the knob moves by itself."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can move several knobs, then <strong>Add</strong> once to capture all of them. To replace a curve, move again (new dots) and <strong>Add</strong>, or tap <strong>X</strong> to keep the previous curve. Parameter menu: <strong>Insert</strong> writes a straight line at the current value; <strong>Delete</strong> removes that parameter’s automation. <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed out while the parameter is automated."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is the link into Level 8, not the full automation chapter. One stored knob move is enough."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that already has notes. Start transport.","Drag one knob (a filter, decay, or <strong>FX1</strong> control).","Confirm a dotted line in <strong>Clip Preview</strong>.","Tap <strong>Add</strong>. Confirm a solid line and a dot on the knob.","Let the clip loop and watch the knob move. If you hate it, open the parameter menu and <strong>Delete</strong>, or <strong>Undo</strong>.","Recite: running + move + dotted + <strong>Add</strong> + solid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you capture automation with transport stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Transport must be running.","What does a dotted line mean versus a solid line? <strong>Answer:</strong> Dotted = pending; solid = stored after <strong>Add</strong>.","Why is Return to Default greyed out? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter is automated."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 and §7.5.1","Capture in Note.md (Automation pages)","Note FAQ (automation recorded via Capture)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Store one filter or FX move on the drum or lead clip. Leave it if it helps the loop. Full automation craft is Level 8."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-5","title":"5.4.5 — When to Quantize after Capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.5 — When to Quantize after Capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize a captured take only when the grid error is the problem you want to fix."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Note Quantize</strong> aligns notes to a musical grid when they were not captured perfectly on beat. Tap once to quantize <strong>all</strong> notes in the clip to the current settings (and select them). Long-press to open the <strong>Quantization</strong> tab: pick a meter value, <strong>Triplets</strong> if needed, and an <strong>Amount</strong> (1.0.15+; syllabus 1.0.16+) from subtle to rigid. <strong>OFF</strong> on a selection returns original positions."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it after Capture when the idea is right but the timing fights the kick or the click. Do not quantize as a reflex. Amount lets you keep some of the take. <strong>Undo</strong> if the snap kills the feel (next lesson)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize is not Capture. It does not add notes. Repeat-captured 1/16 hats are often already on a rate; they may not need a second snap."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Duplicate a captured clip so you still have an unquantized original.","On the copy, tap <strong>Note Quantize</strong> once. Listen against the drums.","<strong>Undo</strong>. Long-press, set a lower <strong>Amount</strong> if available, apply again.","Compare original slot vs copy by launching each.","Keep the version that still sounds like your hands if that was the point.","Recite: Quantize after Capture when timing is the defect, not the idea."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does the manual say to quantize? <strong>Answer:</strong> To correct notes that were not captured perfectly on beat.","What does one tap do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Quantizes all notes to the current settings and selects them.","How do you apply less than a full snap? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Quantization tab <strong>Amount</strong> control (1.0.15 notes / syllabus 1.0.16+)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 and §8.2.3.3","Ableton Note.md §3.3 (Amount slider)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Quantisation amount)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize only the copy of a sloppy melody. Leave drums human unless they actually miss the kick."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-6","title":"5.4.6 — When quantize kills groove","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.6 — When quantize kills groove","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hear when a full quantize erases the feel you meant to keep, and use Groove Amount or a lower amount instead."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize pulls notes toward a grid. Groove is a different control: FAQ and RN <strong>1.0.15</strong> (syllabus 1.0.16+) put <strong>Groove Amount</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong>, with per-clip swing in that release. Groove adds feel; a rigid quantize removes the timing you just played."},{"type":"prose","html":"If hats were captured with Repeat, they are already periodic. If you played them by hand, the small errors may <em>be</em> the groove. A full <strong>Amount</strong> snap can make that line robotic. <strong>Undo</strong>. Try a lower Amount, or leave the take and raise <strong>Groove Amount</strong> instead of snapping harder."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not publish a “never quantize hats” rule or a recommended Amount number. Use your ears. Beatmaking says not to fine-tune an idea until you decide it is worth it — that includes not quantizing a sketch to death."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Keep an unquantized hat or melody clip.","Duplicate it. Fully quantize the copy (high Amount, straight 1/16).","Launch original, then copy. Name what you lost (push, drag, flam).","On the copy, <strong>Undo</strong> or lower Amount. Optionally raise <strong>Set Settings</strong> → <strong>Groove Amount</strong> on the original.","Choose one keeper. Delete or ignore the lifeless copy.","Write one sentence: “I quantize when __; I do not when __.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What Set Settings control adds groove without snapping notes to a grid the Quantize way? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Groove Amount</strong> (1.0.15 notes; plus per-clip swing in that release).","What do you tap if a quantize just ruined a take? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Undo</strong>.","Must every captured clip be quantized? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Only when timing error is the problem."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 / §8.2.3.3 (Quantize purpose; Amount)","Note FAQ (Groove Amount in Set Settings)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Groove Amount + per-clip swing)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (don’t over-fine-tune too early)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A/B the same hat clip at Groove Amount low vs higher, without quantizing. Then decide if that clip still needs Quantize at all."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l5-4-7","title":"5.4.7 — Gate E: drums, bass, and melody captured","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.4.7 — Gate E: drums, bass, and melody captured","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 5 · Module 5.4 — Overdub and layer Capture</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-score Gate E on one Set that holds drums, bass, and melody, each born from Capture."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate E is a checklist: <strong>Fixed Length</strong> used, <strong>Free Length</strong> used, <strong>Capture Timeout</strong> explained, <strong>overdub</strong> done once. The portfolio shape is drums + bass + melody, all captured — not imported as MIDI (impossible) and not only Demo clips."},{"type":"prose","html":"Fixed Length: plus → length → play → <strong>Add</strong> (your 1-bar drums or 4-/8-bar phrase). Free Length: play without a container → <strong>Capture</strong> (stopped, ≤8 bars) or <strong>Create Clip</strong> (running, length ≥ shortest existing). Timeout: you can say on = buffer clears after a short pause; off = buffer lasts through an extended pause until you play again; Timeout does not affect overdub. Overdub: existing clip + transport running + <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Score your Set, not a Demo. Failed line → repeat the lesson → rescore. Do not start Level 6 until all four lines pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Gate E Set. Confirm three tracks with clips you captured: drums, bass, melody.","Point to one Fixed Length clip and name its plus length.","Point to one Free Length clip and say whether you used <strong>Capture</strong> or <strong>Create Clip</strong>.","Overdub one extra layer if you have not yet (running + <strong>Add</strong>).","Say the Timeout on/off rules out loud, including the overdub exception.","Mark pass/fail for the four gate lines. Redo fails now."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What four Gate E lines must pass? <strong>Answer:</strong> Fixed Length used; Free Length used; Capture Timeout explained; overdub once.","What three parts should the portfolio contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drums, bass, and melody, all captured.","May MIDI files replace a missing part? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note cannot import MIDI clips."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Course syllabus Gate E (Level 5 checkpoint)","Ableton Note Manual §2.4.2, §8.1.1, §8.1.2","Capture in Note.md","Note FAQ (MIDI via Capture; no MIDI file I/O)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a four-line pass/fail with a proof under each (which clip, which button, which Timeout setting you use). Do not open Level 6 until every required line passes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l5-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
